.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt ARCANACTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm arcanactl
.Nd command-line client for arcana-agent
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -libxo Ar options
.Op Fl qst
.Op Fl c Ar uri
.Op Fl A Ar cafile Fl C Ar certfile Fl K Ar keyfile
.Op Fl N Ar name
.Ar command
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends one request to a
.Xr arcana-agent 8
and prints the answer.
Output goes through
.Xr libxo 3 ,
so
.Fl -libxo Ns = Ns Ar json
and the other styles work.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A Ar cafile , Fl C Ar certfile , Fl K Ar keyfile
The certificate authority that issued the server's certificate, and
this client's certificate and key, for network URIs.
.It Fl c Ar uri
The daemon to talk to.
Default
.Ql unix:/var/run/arcana-agent.sock .
.It Fl N Ar name
The name the server's certificate must carry, when it differs from the
host in
.Ar uri .
.It Fl F Ar fib
The routing table that
.Cm route
and
.Cm net routes
work in, when not the first.
.It Fl j Ar jail
Work on the network of a running jail that has one of its own
.Pq vnet
instead of the host's: for
.Cm net ,
.Cm route ,
.Cm interface ,
.Cm ng ,
.Cm wg
and
.Cm bird .
The host does it; nothing runs in the jail but the jail's own
.Xr birdc 8 ,
.Xr bird 8
and
.Xr wg 8 .
With
.Fl p
a change is kept in the jail's own
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.It Fl p
Keep a change to an interface or a route in
.Xr rc.conf 5
as well, so that it is there after a boot.
.It Fl q
Queue the change as a job; see the end of the command list.
.It Fl s
Make the change in safe mode; see
.Cm route .
.It Fl t
Also print the trace: every library call, system call, file and
program the daemon used, with the programs' output.
.El
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm hello
Show the host.
.It Cm list Op Fl c
List running and configured jails.
With
.Fl c ,
only configured ones.
.It Cm start | stop | restart | kill Ar jail
Change the jail's state.
.Cm kill
removes the jail without running its stop scripts.
.It Cm address add | remove Oo Fl lnp Oc Oo Fl i Ar interface Oc Ar jail Ar address Ns Op / Ns Ar len
Add or remove an address:
.Fl l
in the running jail (the default),
.Fl p
in its configuration file,
.Fl n
only showing what would change.
.Fl i
names the interface for the alias when the jail has no
.Va interface
parameter.
.It Cm services
The host's rc.d scripts: whether
.Xr rc.conf 5
enables them, and for the enabled ones whether they run, as
.Xr service 8
reports.
.It Cm service Ar name Cm start | stop | restart | reload | enable | disable
Change a service:
.Cm start ,
.Cm stop ,
.Cm restart
and
.Cm reload
through
.Xr service 8 ,
.Cm enable
and
.Cm disable
by setting its rcvar with
.Xr sysrc 8 .
.It Cm service Ar name Cm config Oo Fl x Ar key Oc ... Oo Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value Oc ...
The service's
.Xr rc.conf 5
variables, with what the script says of itself and its extra commands.
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
sets one first,
.Fl x
unsets one; only the service's own variables are accepted.
.It Cm net routes | nexthops | connections | sockets | interfaces | netgraph | wireguard | bird | ipsec Op Fl 4 | Fl 6
The host's routing table
.Pq Nm netstat Fl rnW ,
nexthop table
.Pq Nm netstat Fl onW ,
active connections
.Pq Nm netstat Fl nW
or open sockets
.Pq Nm sockstat Fl w ,
as the program printed them with
.Fl -libxo Cm json ,
for one address family or both.
.It Cm kmod
The kernel's modules and its memory: every file linked into the kernel
with its size, the references to it and the modules it holds; the
modules that could be loaded; what
.Xr rc.conf 5 Ns 's
.Va kld_list
loads at boot; and the largest of the kernel's
.Xr malloc 9
types and
.Xr uma 9
zones.
.It Cm kmod load | unload Ar module
Load a kernel module with
.Xr kldload 8 ,
or unload one with
.Xr kldunload 8 .
The module is named as
.Xr kldload 8
finds it, without a path.
With
.Fl p ,
it is also put into
.Va kld_list ,
or taken out of it.
.It Cm sysctl Op Ar name
The kernel's state, as
.Xr sysctl 8
.Fl a
has it: every variable whose name begins with
.Ar name ,
with its value, its type, whether it can be set and what it is said to
be, and what
.Xr sysctl.conf 5
sets at boot.
Without
.Ar name
the whole tree, of which only the beginning is sent.
.It Cm sysctl parts Op Ar name
The same values as a dump: in parts, each printed as an array of its
own when it comes, with none left out for size.
.It Cm sysctl Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
Set a variable with
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
by a job.
With
.Fl p ,
it is also written to
.Xr sysctl.conf 5 ,
in place of the line that set it before.
.It Cm ps Op Ar pid
What runs on the host, or with
.Fl j
in that jail, as
.Xr top 1
shows it: each process with its user, jail, state, nice value,
threads, memory, the CPU time it has used, its share of a CPU as the
kernel has it
.Pq wcpu
and between this look and the one before
.Pq cpu ,
the blocks it read and wrote and how many a second, when it started,
and its command line; the busiest first; and each jail's share added
up.
With
.Ar pid ,
that process alone, with what it has open and its threads.
.It Cm signal Ar pid Op Ar name | number
Send the process a signal, by a job:
.Dv SIGTERM
when none is named.
Not to
.Xr init 8 ,
the kernel's own processes or the agent; with
.Fl j ,
only to a process of that jail.
.It Cm renice Ar pid Ar nice
Give the process another nice value, from -20 to 20, by a job.
.It Cm log Oo Fl o Ar lines Oc Oo Fl f Ar position Oc Op Ar name Op Ar text
The end of a log in the host's log directory, or with
.Fl j
in the jail's own;
.Li @dmesg ,
the kernel's message buffer, or
.Li @audit ,
the audit trail;
.Pa messages
when none is named, as
.Xr syslogd 8
wrote it: each line with its time, host, tag, pid and text, the logs
there are to read, and the position the file ended at.
With
.Ar text ,
only the lines that have it in them, in either case.
.Fl o
is how many lines to print at most, 200 when not given.
With
.Fl f ,
only what was written after that position, which is how a log is
followed.
A file
.Xr newsyslog 8
has packed is unpacked and read whole.
.It Cm events Op Ar after | Fl f
What happened on the host, as the kernel told the agent of it through
.Xr netlink 4 :
every route, interface, address and neighbour that came, went or
changed, each with its number, time and kind, the last 512 kept.
With
.Ar after ,
only the events of a greater number.
With
.Fl f ,
each event as it happens, a line each, until the host goes or
.Nm
is stopped: the agent tells it to those who subscribed, nothing is
asked again.
.It Cm net nhgroups
The kernel's nexthop groups, what a route of several next hops points
to, each with its next hops and their shares.
.It Cm net neighbours Oo Fl 4 | Fl 6 Oc Op Ar text
The neighbours, those of ARP and of NDP, asked of the kernel through
.Xr netlink 4 :
each with its address, link-level address, interface and state, and
when it moves on; only those with
.Ar text
in them, when given.
.It Cm history Op Ar after
How busy the host has been: what the agent sampled by itself and
kept, each sample with its time, the CPUs' busy share, the load, the
memory and swap in use, and the bytes a second on the network and the
disks, added up and then for each interface and each disk, which are
named, and each running jail's share of one CPU and its memory, as the
kernel's resource accounting has them
.Pq Va kern.racct.enable .
With
.Ar after ,
only the samples after that time, in seconds since the epoch.
.It Cm history set Ar seconds Ar samples Oo Ar cpu mem swap Oc Op Cm rules Ar list
Have the agent sample every
.Ar seconds ,
1 to 3600, and keep that many
.Ar samples ,
at most 5000, from now on and after its next start.
With 0
.Ar samples
it does not sample and holds no memory for it.
With
.Ar cpu ,
.Ar mem
and
.Ar swap ,
the percentages over which an event is made, 0 for none; with
.Cm rules ,
the limits of an interface, a disk or a jail, with commas:
.Ql net: Ns Ar if Ns = Ns Ar bytes/s ,
.Ql disk: Ns Ar disk Ns = Ns Ar bytes/s ,
.Ql jail-cpu: Ns Ar jail Ns = Ns Ar percent
and
.Ql jail-mem: Ns Ar jail Ns = Ns Ar bytes ;
an empty list takes them away.
The reply says what the samples take of the agent's memory.
The samples are written down when the agent stops and read again when
it starts.
.It Cm net routes | connections | sockets Oo Fl 4 | Fl 6 Oc Cm parts Op Ar text
The same table as a dump: in parts, each printed as an array of rows
of its own when it comes, a route with its family in it; none is left
out for size.
.It Cm net bridges | geneve | ipfw
The bridges with their spanning tree and their members' VLANs, the
.Xr geneve 4
tunnels, or the firewall's rules with their sets and counters and the
kept ones.
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm access Ar member Ar vlan
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm trunk Ar member Ar vlan-list Op Ar untagged-vlan
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm vlanfilter on | off
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm flag Ar member Oo Cm - Oc Ns Ar flag
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm set Ar option Ar value
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm ifset Ar member Ar option Ar value
.It Cm bridge Ar name Cm addm | deletem Ar member
Change an
.Xr if_bridge 4 ,
by a job.
An access port is in one VLAN untagged and carries none tagged; a trunk
carries a list of VLANs tagged
.Pq Cm none , all , No or numbers and ranges with commas between
and may have one untagged.
A flag is one of stp, edge, autoedge, ptp, autoptp, learn, discover,
sticky, private, span, qinq.
With
.Fl p
it is kept in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
A member of a bridge is given no address.
.It Cm geneve Ar name Cm create | set Ar option Ar value ...
.It Cm geneve Ar name Cm flush Op Cm all
Make a
.Xr geneve 4
tunnel, or change one, by a job.
The options are mode
.Pq l2 or l3, of a new one only ,
id, local, remote, group, dev, localport, remoteport, portrange, ttl
.Pq a number or inherit ,
df
.Pq set, unset or inherit ,
maxaddr, timeout, and learn, external and dscpinherit, each on or off.
.It Cm ipfw Oo Cm keep Oc Cm add Ar number Ar rule ...
.It Cm ipfw Oo Cm keep Oc Cm delete Ar number
A rule of the firewall, by a job: added to or deleted from what the
kernel has now; with
.Fl p
also the kept rules; after
.Cm keep
only those.
.It Cm ipfw apply
Make the kept rules the whole ruleset without a gap: loaded into set 1
while it is disabled, then swapped with set 0.
With
.Fl s
the rules that were there wait in set 1 and are swapped back unless the
job is confirmed.
.It Cm ipfw enable | disable | zero Op Ar number
Turn the firewall on or off, with
.Fl p
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
too, or clear the counters.
.It Cm ipfw table | nat | nat64lsn | nat64stl | nat64clat | nptv6 | pipe | queue | sched Ar name Ar word ...
What a rule names and is not one, in
.Xr ipfw 8 Ns 's
own words:
.Li table T46 add 10.0.0.1 ,
.Li nat 1 config if em0 ,
.Li pipe 1 config bw 10Mbit/s .
With
.Fl p ,
the same words are also a line at the head of the kept rules.
With
.Fl j ,
of that jail's own firewall, as every
.Cm ipfw
command is: ipfw is run in the jail.
.It Cm ipfw rules Oo Fl r | Fl w Ar file Oo Fl n Oc Oc
The kept rules, a file of
.Xr ipfw 8
commands: printed, or with
.Fl w
replaced by
.Ar file
once ipfw has read it without doing it; with
.Fl n
only checked.
With
.Fl r ,
the file
.Xr rc.conf 5 Ns 's
.Va firewall_type
names, which the firewall loads now, printed as kept rules: what
applying does itself left in as comments, nothing written.
.It Cm ipfw trace Ar address Op Ar seconds Op Ar packets
Packets to or from
.Ar address
followed by the kernel itself: for each, the rule of the firewall that
decided it and what it decided, its interface and direction, as the
kernel's
.Ql ipfw:::rule-matched
probe tells
.Xr dtrace 1 ;
for 10
.Ar seconds
or 100
.Ar packets ,
whichever ends first.
With
.Fl j ,
of the jail's own firewall.
The host needs
.Xr dtrace 4
loaded.
.It Cm transport Op Cm retry
How the agent is reached: its listeners, each up or not and why not,
whether the kernel has SCTP, and the connections open to the agent now.
With
.Cm retry
the agent starts the SCTP listeners that are not up, which it does not
do by itself once the kernel had no SCTP when it started.
.It Cm storage health
How each disk says it is: asked with
.Xr nvmecontrol 8
when it is an NVMe disk, and with
.Xr smartctl 8
otherwise, when the host has it.
.It Cm zfs load-key | unload-key Oo Fl r Oc Ar dataset
Load the key of an encrypted dataset from where its
.Va keylocation
says, or forget it.
.It Cm zfs jail | unjail Ar dataset Ar jail
Give a dataset to a running jail to manage as its own, or take it back.
.It Cm zfs resnapshot Oo Fl r Oc Ar snapshot
The newest snapshot of its dataset made again, now, under the same
name: the new one is taken under a name of its own, the old one
destroyed, and the new one renamed to it, so that there is a snapshot
all along.
One that is not the newest is refused; one that
.Xr zfs 8
will not destroy (held, cloned) stays as it was.
.It Cm zfs replicate Oo Fl r Oc Ar snapshot Ar new-dataset Op Ar since-snapshot
Send a snapshot into a dataset of the same host that is not there yet;
with
.Ar since-snapshot ,
only what changed since that one, which both have.
What arrives is not mounted.
.It Cm gpart newfs Oo Fl f Oc Ar partition Op Ar label
Make a UFS file system on a partition.
Refused while it is mounted, swapped on or a pool's device, and,
without
.Fl f ,
while it has a file system on it.
.It Cm gpart growfs Ar partition
Grow a partition's UFS file system to the partition's size.
.It Cm swapon | swapoff Ar device
Swap on a device, or no longer.
.It Cm storage disks | pools | datasets | mounts
.It Cm storage snapshots Op Ar dataset
The host's storage, as JSON: the disks and their partitions as GEOM
has them, the ZFS pools with their devices, the datasets, the
snapshots (the newest first, of all or of one dataset and those under
it), and what is mounted beside what
.Xr fstab 5
says to mount.
.It Cm zfs snapshot | destroy | rollback Oo Fl r Oc Ar name
.It Cm zfs create Ar dataset Op Ar property Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.It Cm zfs set Ar dataset Ar property Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.It Cm zfs inherit Ar dataset Ar property
.It Cm zfs mount | unmount Oo Fl f Oc | promote Ar dataset
.It Cm zfs clone Ar snapshot Ar dataset
.It Cm zfs rename Ar name Ar name
.Xr zfs 8 ,
by a job.
A dataset is made a volume by
.Li volsize= Ns Ar size .
A pool's own dataset is not destroyed.
.It Cm zpool scrub | scrub-stop | trim Ar pool
.It Cm zpool clear Ar pool Op Ar device
.It Cm zpool online | offline Ar pool Ar device
.It Cm zpool create Oo Fl f Oc Ar pool Oo Cm mirror | raidz1 | raidz2 | raidz3 Oc Ar device ... Op Cm mountpoint= Ns Ar path
.It Cm zpool add Oo Fl f Oc Ar pool Oo Cm mirror | log | cache | spare | special Oc Ar device ...
.It Cm zpool attach Ar pool Ar device Ar new-device
.It Cm zpool detach | remove Ar pool Ar device
.It Cm zpool replace Ar pool Ar device Op Ar new-device
.It Cm zpool export | destroy Ar pool
.It Cm zpool import Ar pool Op Cm altroot= Ns Ar path
.Xr zpool 8 ,
by a job.
A pool with a dataset mounted where the system lives, the root or
under
.Pa /usr ,
.Pa /var ,
.Pa /home
and the like, is not destroyed or exported, and no pool is by force.
.Cm storage importable
lists the pools that could be imported.
.It Cm mount Ar path
Mount what
.Xr fstab 5
describes for
.Ar path .
.It Cm umount Oo Fl f Oc Ar path
Unmount what is mounted there; never the root.
.It Cm gpart create Ar disk Cm gpt | mbr | bsd
.It Cm gpart add Ar disk Ar type Op Ar size Op Ar label
.It Cm gpart delete Ar disk Ar index
.It Cm gpart resize Ar disk Ar index Op Ar size
.It Cm gpart modify Ar disk Ar index Ar label
.It Cm gpart destroy Ar disk
.Xr gpart 8 ,
by a job.
Partitions are aligned to 1 MiB; a table is destroyed only when it is
empty.
.It Cm cam rescan
.Ql camcontrol rescan all .
.It Cm fstab Op Fl w Ar new-text Op Fl n
Print
.Xr fstab 5 ,
or put the text of the local file
.Ar new-text
in its place: taken only when every line is an entry, and the one
before is kept beside it.
With
.Fl n
it is only checked.
.Pp
In all of these the host checks every word before a program is given
it; none is ever an option, and none is done in safe mode.
.It Cm stats
CPU busy share since the previous request, load averages, memory, swap
and the ZFS pools.
.It Cm packages Oo Fl i Oc Oo Fl r Oc Oo Fl 4 | Fl 6 Oc Op Ar jail
Packages of the host, or of the running
.Ar jail ,
that the repository has newer versions of
.Pq Nm pkg version Fl vRl '<' ;
.Fl r
fetches the catalogue first
.Pq Nm pkg update .
With
.Fl i ,
the packages installed instead, from the host's own database without
the repository
.Pq Nm pkg query :
each with its version, whether it was installed by hand or
automatically as what another needs, its size and what it is.
.Fl 4
or
.Fl 6
has pkg reach the repository over IPv4 or IPv6 only.
.It Cm pkg-upgrade Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl O Oc Oo Fl 4 | Fl 6 Oc Oo Fl j Ar jail Oc Op Ar package ...
.Nm pkg upgrade Fl y
of the named packages, or of all of them, on the host or in the running
.Ar jail ;
.Fl n
only shows what would be done;
.Fl O
also installs packages built for a newer FreeBSD than the host's
.Pq Ev IGNORE_OSVERSION ,
which pkg refuses otherwise.
What is still pending follows.
.It Cm pkg-clean Oo Fl a Oc Oo Fl j Ar jail | Fl J Oc
.Nm pkg clean Fl y ,
a job: the downloaded packages no longer needed, or no longer in the
repository, deleted from the cache of the host or the running
.Ar jail ;
with
.Fl a ,
all of the cache.
With
.Fl J ,
in every running jail instead, one batch of a job each, so that a jail
whose pkg refuses fails alone.
.It Cm pkg-install Oo Fl O Oc Oo Fl 4 | Fl 6 Oc Oo Fl j Ar jail Oc Ar package ...
.Nm pkg install Fl y ,
a job, on the host or in the running
.Ar jail ;
with
.Fl O ,
also packages built for a newer FreeBSD.
.It Cm jobs
The host's queued, running and recently finished jobs.
.It Cm job Oo Fl w Oc Ar id
One job with the trace of what it has done so far;
.Fl w
waits until it has finished.
.It Cm job-cancel Ar id
Take a job that has not started out of the queue.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm up | down
Bring a network interface up or down.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm add | remove Ar address
Add an IPv4 or IPv6
.Ar address ,
with its prefix length, to an interface as an alias, or take it off.
.It Cm route add Ar destination gateway Oo Cm metric Ar n Oc Oo Cm weight Ar n Oc Oo Cm source Ar address Oc
Add a static route to
.Ar destination ,
which is
.Li default ,
a network with its prefix length or a host address, by
.Ar gateway ,
an address or the name of an interface, or several addresses with a
comma between, each with its share after an @
.Pq Li 10.0.0.1@2,10.0.0.2 ,
for one route with as many next hops.
The kernel is asked through
.Xr netlink 4 .
.Li ::/0
is the default route of IPv6.
.Cm metric
is the route's cost, the lowest of several to one destination being the
one used;
.Cm weight
its share among routes of one cost; and
.Cm source
the address of the host that what takes the route is sent from.
The routing table is that of the option
.Fl F .
.It Cm route delete Ar destination Oo Ar gateway Oc Oo Cm metric Ar n Oc
Delete the route to
.Ar destination .
.It Cm route get Ar address
The route the host would send a packet to
.Ar address
by, as
.Xr route 8
get tells it, and the other routes of the table that cover the address,
each with the length of its prefix: what is left when the best goes.
.It Cm traceroute Ar address
One round of
.Xr traceroute 8 ,
or
.Xr traceroute6 8 ,
from the host to
.Ar address :
a probe a hop, a second's wait, 30 hops at most, as JSON.
The console's Traceroute asks again and again, as
.Xr mtr 8
does.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm mtu Ar number
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm description Ar text
Set an interface's MTU or its description; an empty text takes the
description off.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm create Oo Cm tunnel Ar local remote Oc Oo Cm address Ar address Oo Cm peer Ar address Oc Oc Oo Cm mtu Ar number Oc Oo Cm description Ar text Oc Oo Cm id Ar number Oc Oo Cm parent Ar interface Oc Oo Cm reqid Ar number Oc
Create a cloned interface and set it up.
.Ar name
is a type with its unit, as
.Ql gif0 ,
or a type alone, for the kernel to pick the unit: epair, gif, gre,
ipsec, me, vxlan, vlan, bridge, lagg, tap, tun, lo, wg or ovpn.
.Cm tunnel
gives a tunnel's outer ends,
.Cm address
and
.Cm peer
its inner ones,
.Cm id
a VLAN's tag or a VXLAN's identifier, and
.Cm parent
the interface a VLAN rides or a bridge takes in, and
.Cm reqid
the reqid of an
.Xr ipsec 4
interface, from 1 to 16383, for the IPsec connection that serves it.
A tunnel's ends are both IPv4 or both IPv6.
What cannot be finished is destroyed again.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm give | take Ar jail
Give the host's interface to a running jail that has a network of its
own, or take it back to the host.
With
.Fl p ,
also as
.Va vnet.interface
of the jail in
.Xr jail.conf 5 ,
so the jail has it again when it next starts.
.It Cm interface epair give Ar jail Op Ar bridge
Make a new
.Xr epair 4
for the jail: its
.Sy b
end goes to the jail, its
.Sy a
end stays on the host, brought up as a member of
.Ar bridge
when one is named.
.It Cm interface Ar name Cm destroy
Destroy a cloned interface.
.It Cm ng mkpeer | connect | rmhook | shutdown | name Ar argument ...
Change the
.Xr netgraph 4
graph: the command and its arguments are given to
.Xr ngctl 8 .
.It Cm wg Ar interface Cm init Op Ar port
Give a WireGuard interface a private key, made on the host, when it has
none, and with
.Ar port
the port it listens on.
The key never leaves the host:
.Cm net wireguard
shows public keys only.
.It Cm wg Ar interface Cm peer Ar key Oo Cm endpoint Ar address : Ns Ar port Oc Oo Cm allowed-ips Ar list Oc Oo Cm keepalive Ar seconds Oc
Add the peer whose public key is
.Ar key ,
or change it.
.It Cm wg Ar interface Cm unpeer Ar key
Remove a peer.
.It Cm ipsec init
Give the host an IPsec key of its own, ECDSA P-256, made there, when it
has none; its private half never leaves the host, and
.Cm net ipsec
tells its public half, in base64, for its peers.
With
.Fl p ,
strongSwan is also started at boot.
.It Cm ipsec start
Start strongSwan's daemon.
.It Cm ipsec conn Ar name local remote Cm tunnel | transport Ar pubkey Oo Cm reqid Ar n Oc Oo Cm local-ts Ar list Oc Oo Cm remote-ts Ar list Oc Oo Cm start Cm start | trap | none Oc Oo Cm local-id Ar id Oc Oo Cm remote-id Ar id Oc
Write the IPsec connection
.Ar name ,
IKEv2 only, between the addresses
.Ar local
and
.Ar remote ,
authenticated by this host's key and the peer's,
.Ar pubkey ,
with one child in tunnel or transport mode between the traffic
selectors given, by default the two addresses.
With
.Cm reqid
it serves the
.Xr ipsec 4
interface of that reqid: tunnel mode, its policies the interface's.
strongSwan reads it at once; if it refuses, what was there before is
put back.
.It Cm ipsec unconn | up | down Ar name
Take the connection away, its security associations ended first; start
them; end them.
.Pp
With
.Fl j ,
in the vnet jail named, whose own strongSwan it is.
.Pp
With
.Fl p
the interface as it is then, its key, addresses and peers, is written
to
.Pa /usr/local/etc/wireguard/ Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .conf
for
